269
00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.220
so that's pretty reasonable.

270
00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.780
There was only a 13% decrease in strength

271
00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.350
after taking those 30 weeks off.

272
00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:23.350
That shows that this strength is very resistant to dropping

273
00:09:24.837 --> 00:09:28.600
after only decreasing 13% in 30 weeks.

274
00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.610
There is other data that shows a bit more,

275
00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:31.990
and I do think for the most part

276
00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:34.280
you would probably lose a bit more.

277
00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:37.080
So nobody should take that 13% as an absolute number.

278
00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.502
But, I do think that it shows the amount

279
00:09:40.502 --> 00:09:44.620
that you lose isn't going to be some sort of astronomical

280
00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.674
amount if you have to take a few months off.

281
00:09:46.674 --> 00:09:49.960
That's just going to be killer for a long period of time.

282
00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:51.100
And then when they started the training again

283
00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:51.933
after six weeks,

284
00:09:51.933 --> 00:09:53.830
not only did they get back to where they were

285
00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:55.360
after the 20 weeks of training,

286
00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.500
but they even got a little bit better than that.

287
00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.330
But the main point being following a large increase

288
00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:03.110
in strength after a long period of really good training,

289
00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:04.790
if you take some months off,

290
00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:06.490
in this case it was 30 weeks,

291
00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:08.210
you are gonna see a decrease in strength,

292
00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:10.350
but it's not going to be a ton.

293
00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:12.010
You will be able to make that up

294
00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.190
in a couple months when you come back.

295
00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:17.446
So strength and size are pretty resistant to decline.

296
00:10:17.446 --> 00:10:19.117
Now, this is from that same study

297
00:10:19.117 --> 00:10:20.440
and that same group of women,

298
00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.220
and it was looking at hypertrophy as well.

299
00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:23.550
So we're gonna see results here

300
00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:25.140
similar to the Blazevich study

301
00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.530
where if we just scroll back very quickly two slides,

302
00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:32.123
we can see a decrease in hypertrophy after three months,

303
00:10:32.123 --> 00:10:35.600
decrease in hypertrophy, or a decrease in muscle thickness,

304
00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.704
but it's not significant, and it's not a lot.

305
00:10:37.704 --> 00:10:40.430
If we go back to the Staron study now,

306
00:10:40.430 --> 00:10:44.070
we can see this is showing from PRE to 20 weeks of training

307
00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.870
and then 20 weeks of training

308
00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:50.230
down to after the 30 weeks of detraining.

309
00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:53.960
So this is type 1 fibers, type 2,

310
00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.330
and then type 2B, type 2X.

311
00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.480
So, if we're looking at this here,

312
00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:01.330
we can see a 15% increase in size,

313
00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:05.394
a 39% and a 47%, so that's what these numbers are.

314
00:11:05.394 --> 00:11:08.350
15%, 39 and 47.

315
00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:12.190
And then over here, we're seeing the decreases

316
00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:13.340
after the detraining.

317
00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:15.892
So type one fibers down 1%,

318
00:11:15.892 --> 00:11:17.796
and then here we're down 10%

319
00:11:17.796 --> 00:11:19.860
and then here we're down 14%.

320
00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:22.490
So clearly this is tending to decrease.

321
00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:24.895
The only significant decrease was this 14%

322
00:11:24.895 --> 00:11:26.330
but it does look like

323
00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.810
there's certainly a clear trend for this.

324
00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:31.130
Type 2 fibers to decrease 10% obviously

325
00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:32.110
if you would take more time off,

326
00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:34.360
if would likely be a significant decrease.

327
00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:37.407
But nonetheless, we're seeing a small decrease here

328
00:11:37.407 --> 00:11:39.170
after these 30 weeks off,

329
00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.310
but then we they start to train again for six weeks,

330
00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:43.630
we're seeing the hypertrophy occur again,

331
00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:45.410
muscle mass increase again.

332
00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:49.420
So it just goes to drive home the point again,

333
00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:50.970
as we saw in the previous couple slides

334
00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:53.730
that yes you are gonna lose size and strength

335
00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:55.050
but it's not gonna be killer

336
00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:56.560
and this has been seen for a long time,

337
00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:58.450
if we go back to this '91 study,

338
00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:00.100
and then we go back to the Blazevich study

339
00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:02.730
that was done in about the past decade or so.

340
00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:04.460
So the data is in agreement there.

341
00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:07.570
Now lastly, I wanna talk about briefly

342
00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:09.200
before we go into our sample program,

343
00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:10.380
why this occurs.

344
00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:12.880
And there's going to be some conflicting evidence on this,

345
00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:14.830
so I wanna show this one paper here,

346
00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:17.127
'cause this is one of the more foundational papers

347
00:12:17.127 --> 00:12:18.600
from 2004,

348
00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:19.950
but there is a paper that's just out

349
00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:22.490
really recently in the last month or so

350
00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:23.790
that is questioning

351
00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:27.510
this hypothesis or these findings.

352
00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:29.610
So I think there is conflicting data in the area,

353
00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.480
but nonetheless, we've covered in MASS before

354
00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:33.560
so if you can think back

355
00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:36.836
to the concept of myonuclear addition.

356
00:12:36.836 --> 00:12:39.500
So with myonuclear addition,

357
00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.170
we've talked about how hypertrophy occurs

358
00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:43.760
one because of protein synthesis,

359
00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:46.740
but two because when satellite cells are activated,

360
00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:49.954
a nuclei from satellite cells can be donated to a myofiber,

361
00:12:49.954 --> 00:12:53.117
and when a myofiber has more myonuclei,

362
00:12:53.117 --> 00:12:57.190
it tends to support a larger area, if you will.

363
00:12:57.190 --> 00:12:59.600
Each nuclei can only support so much domain

364
00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:00.700
or so much area,

365
00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:02.500
so when an additional nuclei is added,

366
00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.910
it can support more area and the myofiber can grow.

367
00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.750
So that comes from satellite cells.

368
00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:09.350
So when there's more satellite cells

369
00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:11.760
around the myofiber, that tends to mean

370
00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:14.340
that individual has a greater propensity for growth.

371
00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.970
Someone that has a fewer amount of satellite cells

372
00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:20.270
around a myofiber, has a lower propensity for growth,

373
00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:22.880
it might be a low responder to training.

374
00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:24.090
A higher amount of satellite cells

375
00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:25.890
might be a higher responder to training.

376
00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:29.320
So with that said, this study from Kadi in 2004

377
00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.340
looks at 90 days of training

378
00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:33.787
and 90 days of detraining in men.

379
00:13:33.787 --> 00:13:36.620
And so, if we look at fiber area,

380
00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:39.150
or muscle size, hypertrophy here,

381
00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:44.150
we can see hypertrophy from PRE to 90 days of training.

382
00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:45.899
Now, after the detraining,

383
00:13:45.899 --> 00:13:48.882
we can see a decrease in fiber size.

384
00:13:48.882 --> 00:13:51.910
That's all the way up to the 90 days detraining.

385
00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:53.810
So at 90 days detraining here,

386
00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:55.750
this is significantly lower than it was

387
00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:58.000
when they ended the 90 days of training.

388
00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.380
If we go over to the right,

389
00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:01.730
we're gonna see a somewhat similar graph

390
00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:04.370
when looking at satellite cell number per fiber.

391
00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:05.203
So if you remember,

392
00:14:05.203 --> 00:14:06.780
we just talked about how satellite cells,

393
00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:08.520
somebody has more satellite cells

394
00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.290
they can donate more myonuclei,

395
00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:12.234
have a greater propensity for growth.

396
00:14:12.234 --> 00:14:15.004
So, we see an increase in the number of satellite cells

397
00:14:15.004 --> 00:14:16.910
per fiber at 90 days,

398
00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:17.960
but a decrease here,

399
00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:20.190
close to back to baseline, right?

400
00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:22.490
After 90 days of detraining.

401
00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:25.390
So after 90 days of detraining here,

402
00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:28.800
we are seeing that and that this bar here

403
00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:30.120
is pretty similar to this one,

404
00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:31.875
so a pretty similar trend here.

405
00:14:31.875 --> 00:14:34.400
Up and down and up and down.

406
00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:35.770
So the authors noted here

407
00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.730
that one of the reason that

408
00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:42.710
muscle size might go down here in the detraining

409
00:14:42.710 --> 00:14:44.270
is because of satellite cell number

410
00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:46.700
and thus myonuclei number going down.

411
00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:47.960
So, that being said,

412
00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:49.390
there is some conflicting evidence

413
00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.110
on the maintenance of myonuclei.

414
00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:55.385
Some data is showing that even during a time of detraining

415
00:14:55.385 --> 00:14:57.950
and when there is a loss in muscle size,

416
00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:01.080
that myonuclei number remains similar or doesn't

417
00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.740
go all the way back down to baseline

418
00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:04.890
and this data does show that myonuclei number

419
00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:07.520
or satellite cell number falls pretty far.

420
00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:09.860
So, the decrease in satellite cell number

421
00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:12.000
or myonuclei could be a mechanism

422
00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:15.610
that causes a decrease in hypertrophy with detraining,

423
00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:17.750
but we're not 100% sure on that.

424
00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:19.830
But this is hopefully helping to explain

425
00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:22.570
or to give you one hypothesis why that may occur.

426
00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:25.300
So if you could maintain your satellite cell number

427
00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:26.140
during detraining,

428
00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:28.170
hopefully that could help maintain muscle size,

429
00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:30.240
but again we're not entirely sure.

430
00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:31.670
So where does this leave you?

431
00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:34.680
Forgetting the mechanistic stuff for a moment,

432
00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:36.170
the decrease in muscle size

433
00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:38.096
and the decreased strength that occurred,

434
00:15:38.096 --> 00:15:40.260
puts you not back to baseline

435
00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:42.430
and then regaining pretty quickly.

436
00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:44.433
Meaning, you do decrease muscle size

437
00:15:44.433 --> 00:15:46.080
and you do decrease strength

438
00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:47.660
but you can make that back.

439
00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:50.750
So all the data does show quicker regaining,

440
00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:52.520
specifically the Staron study,

441
00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:54.837
although they only took 30 weeks of detraining

442
00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:57.720
and only six weeks of training

443
00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:00.020
which is why I think that was a bit fast

444
00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:01.080
in how they gained it,

445
00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:02.880
but I would take into account training status

446
00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.720
and say in the Staron study

447
00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:06.890
the women were trained, but not super well-trained,

448
00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:09.320
so they probably regained it a little more quickly.

449
00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:11.260
But, something to be cautious,

450
00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:13.070
we'll say if you take four or five months off,

451
00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:16.720
and this is using the data and being a little bit cautious

452
00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:17.553
on my part,

453
00:16:17.553 --> 00:16:20.390
so it's not an exact exact timeframe,

454
00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:21.980
but it's based upon the data

455
00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:24.937
and then saying, "Hey, just to make sure

456
00:16:24.937 --> 00:16:27.077
"nobody leaves this video thinking they can gain

457
00:16:27.077 --> 00:16:28.740
"all their strength back in a week."

458
00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:30.030
being a little cautious.

459
00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:31.380
So we take four to five months off,

460
00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:34.030
it probably takes about two to four months to regain.

461
00:16:34.030 --> 00:16:35.328
So about three months off,

462
00:16:35.328 --> 00:16:37.840
two three months to be able to regain.

463
00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:39.080
So in the grand scheme of things,

464
00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:40.410
it's really not that much time.

465
00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:43.500
I try to stress a lot and think about training

466
00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:46.240
in terms of let's say 20 years or 25 years, right?

467
00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:48.280
If you're in this, and you're a member of MASS

468
00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:49.650
training is important to you,

469
00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:50.830
and it's something that you're probably gonna do

470
00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:52.140
for a long time,

471
00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:53.107
and so in that case I would say

472
00:16:53.107 --> 00:16:55.099
if you have to take a few months off now,

473
00:16:55.099 --> 00:16:57.739
but you know, you have 20 more years to train,

474
00:16:57.739 --> 00:16:59.880
it's really not gonna matter that much.

475
00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:01.730
Just as last month we talked about

476
00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:03.320
if you wanted to run a marathon,

477
00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:05.072
certainly that's not gonna be ideal

478
00:17:05.072 --> 00:17:06.990
for your power lifting progress

479
00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:08.090
or for your body building progress

480
00:17:08.090 --> 00:17:09.100
in the short term.

481
00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:10.723
But if you have 20 more years to train,

482
00:17:10.723 --> 00:17:11.960
you're gonna be fine.

483
00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.930
You might as well do it and have some fun.

484
00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:18.240
So, mechanistically, we talked about possible contributions:

485
00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:20.070
satellite cell and myonuclei number,

486
00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:22.030
but certainly there's neuromuscular changes,

487
00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:23.530
fiber type adaptations.

488
00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:25.600
Not only the decrease in size,

489
00:17:25.600 --> 00:17:27.135
but from a strength perspective,

490
00:17:27.135 --> 00:17:29.570
the shifting of the fiber interconversions

491
00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:30.620
that you're gonna see

492
00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:35.740
away from type 2, 2X or type 1 if you stop training

493
00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:38.670
and then with a loss of skill adaptation,

494
00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:39.660
I'm sure we've seen this,

495
00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:40.590
if you don't squat for a while,

496
00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:41.500
you don't deadlift for a while,

497
00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:42.830
you don't bench for a while,

498
00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:43.663
you go back to it,

499
00:17:43.663 --> 00:17:45.690
the movement just feels uncomfortable at first.

500
00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:48.129
The rate of force development's probably lower as well.

501
00:17:48.129 --> 00:17:50.307
So all of those things are gonna harm strength

502
00:17:50.307 --> 00:17:52.810
which is why I suggest that the skilled lifts

503
00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:55.360
may see a greater detriment in strength

504
00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:57.770
from time off than the non skilled lifts

505
00:17:57.770 --> 00:17:59.680
if you're doing a leg extension or a leg press,

506
00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:01.860
or something like that, it doesn't take as much skill

507
00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:04.780
as a squat, so you don't have the skill aspect

508
00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:06.170
of it in terms of technique,

509
00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:08.570
all you have is the neuromuscular aspect of it.

510
00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:09.950
So where does this leave you?

511
00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:11.450
It leaves in the fact that you're gonna lose

512
00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:12.500
some size and strength

513
00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:14.800
but it's not gonna be as much as you think.

514
00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:16.930
But, you also need to consider

515
00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:18.025
that when you return,

516
00:18:18.025 --> 00:18:20.600
even if you haven't lost as much strength

517
00:18:20.600 --> 00:18:21.433
as you thought,

518
00:18:21.433 --> 00:18:23.080
or as much size as you thought.

519
00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:25.931
You're going to be more susceptible to muscle damage.

520
00:18:25.931 --> 00:18:28.150
Meaning you're gonna have greater fatigue

521
00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:30.550
that occurs when you start to train again, right?

522
00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:32.480
And your neural adaptations have diminished

523
00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:34.280
and they can be regained quickly.

524
00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:35.700
But if we just focus on these top two,

525
00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:37.130
damage and fatigue,

526
00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:40.500
even if you haven't lost as much strength as you thought,

527
00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:42.084
or as much size as you thought,

528
00:18:42.084 --> 00:18:43.916
when you come back to training,

529
00:18:43.916 --> 00:18:46.450
you haven't done it for three months.

530
00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:49.080
So if you haven't done something for three months,

531
00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:52.670
it's going to fatigue you a lot more easily

532
00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:53.780
than it used to.

533
00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:55.930
You're not completely back to baseline.

534
00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:58.040
After one training bout, the repeated bout effect

535
00:18:58.040 --> 00:18:59.724
can last for up to six months, right?

536
00:18:59.724 --> 00:19:03.140
But the degree to which the repeated bout effect occurs

537
00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:04.900
from today to next week

538
00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:07.240
is a lot greater than the degree which is occurs

539
00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:09.090
from today to three months from now.

540
00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:11.220
So you're going to be susceptible to damage,

541
00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.310
so you have to take that into account.

542
00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:15.853
You're gonna wanna start back with very little.

543
00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:18.330
If you did nothing,

544
00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.170
and I like to say this a lot too,

545
00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:21.710
if you did nothing for three months

546
00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:25.190
and now you just do something, it will work.

547
00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:27.670
And so what we're gonna propose here in the sample program

548
00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:30.890
at first isn't gonna seem like very much training.

549
00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:33.360
But remember, doing nothing and now doing something

550
00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:34.193
is a lot.

551
00:19:34.193 --> 00:19:35.860
I always give the example,

552
00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.730
you know, I went a long time without ever playing basketball

553
00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:39.890
or shooting free throws.

554
00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:41.550
I shoot free throws in my drive way

555
00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:42.660
every single day now,

556
00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:46.310
I'm still terrible, but I'm infinitely better

557
00:19:46.310 --> 00:19:48.120
than I was a year ago.

558
00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.150
Because now I'm actually playing, right?

559
00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:53.290
So I was doing nothing and now I'm doing something.

560
00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:54.123
Right?

561
00:19:54.123 --> 00:19:56.120
I'm still terrible, but relative to where I was,

562
00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:57.090
I'm better.

563
00:19:57.090 --> 00:19:58.600
So if you don't lift for three months

564
00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:00.720
and then you just do a little bit,

565
00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:02.430
you will make progress.

566
00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:05.250
So, your previous numbers don't matter,

567
00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:06.920
you shouldn't use percentages to start,

568
00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:08.240
'cause you don't know where your maxes are,

569
00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:09.510
we can estimate.

570
00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:12.140
You can use RPE, but even at first,

571
00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:14.380
I don't wanna run the risk of overshooting it too much,

572
00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:15.550
so what I like to do,

573
00:20:15.550 --> 00:20:18.280
is just to program absurdly light loads.

574
00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:19.940
So when we go through this program,

575
00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:21.750
we're gonna give an example program

576
00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:23.740
and we're gonna give what this person's numbers were

577
00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:27.170
before they went on their three-month hiatus,

578
00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:30.730
and then we're gonna give you the absolute numbers

579
00:20:30.730 --> 00:20:31.563
in this program

580
00:20:31.563 --> 00:20:32.830
and you're gonna see how light they are

581
00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:35.990
and we'll go through a 12-week program to get us back.

582
00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:37.329
So the goal with this program,

583
00:20:37.329 --> 00:20:39.550
or 13-week program, I should say,

584
00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:41.090
I ended up adding a 13th week

585
00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:42.950
'cause I wanted to get a little taper in there.

586
00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:45.570
But a 13-week retraining program

587
00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:47.700
following 12 weeks or three months of detraining,

588
00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:49.673
so roughly three months of detraining

589
00:20:49.673 --> 00:20:52.360
and then roughly three months of retraining.

590
00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:54.920
So during this three months of retraining,

591
00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:58.125
this might not get you all the way back, all right?

592
00:20:58.125 --> 00:21:00.480
But it could get you pretty close,

593
00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:02.360
and remember this is just one example.

594
00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:04.340
So anytime I put together a sample program

595
00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:05.570
I think it's always imperative,

596
00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:08.470
just as a reminder that I'm just giving an example

597
00:21:08.470 --> 00:21:11.140
of how to do things, this isn't the only way to do it.

598
00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:12.580
This isn't the exact program

599
00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:14.110
that I would write for everyone,

600
00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:15.800
and I think everyone here knows that,

601
00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:19.137
it's just rather an example of saying,

602
00:21:19.137 --> 00:21:20.785
"Hey, if this is what you've done,"

603
00:21:20.785 --> 00:21:24.310
and it's taking a specific person, right?

604
00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:26.010
This person doesn't actually exist,

605
00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:27.970
but I've created a training block

606
00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:29.100
that this person was doing

607
00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:31.490
before they went on their three month hiatus.

608
00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:32.810
So based upon that,

609
00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:35.290
that's how we're creating our retraining program.

610
00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:36.770
So whatever program you were doing,

611
00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:38.370
or whatever level you were at,

612
00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.010
you would wanna take all of these principles

613
00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:41.550
and apply them to you.

614
00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:43.980
So, our pre-detraining program

615
00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:45.580
that we're gonna see in a moment.

616
00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:46.750
This individual is benching

617
00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:48.370
and squatting three times per week,

618
00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:50.720
about 12 sets and 10 sets respectively,

619
00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:52.640
and then deadlifting twice a week for eight sets

620
00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:54.670
and then appropriate assistance work and so forth.

621
00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:57.010
So about 10 more sets after that per muscle group,

622
00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:59.300
around 20 sets or so for some of the muscle groups,

623
00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:01.280
a little more, a little less for others.

624
00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.597
This individual we're gonna say they have a dual focus

625
00:22:03.597 --> 00:22:05.750
they definitely wanna get stronger,

626
00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:07.460
but they've got a lot of volume in there,

627
00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:09.070
they're definitely focused on hypertrophy as well.

628
00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:11.060
So kind of a dual focus, here.

629
00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:13.440
So this is the pre-detraining program.

630
00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:15.849
This is training a lot, training six days per week.

631
00:22:15.849 --> 00:22:18.770
You can see, benching three times a week as we said,

632
00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:21.040
Monday, Wednesday and Friday.

633
00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:26.040
And then squatting Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday

634
00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:29.250
in an undulating fashion, RPE for some lifts,

635
00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:31.700
using some various assistance movements,

636
00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:33.960
and all of the programming tools

637
00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:35.460
that we've talked about in MASS so far,

638
00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:36.780
they're all in this program,

639
00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:38.620
it's a very similar starting program

640
00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:40.220
to what I showed last month

641
00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:42.480
in the concurrent marathon video,

642
00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:45.010
and then off of that we worked the program

643
00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:46.490
to get ready for the marathon,

644
00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:48.710
off of this, this kind of standard program

645
00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:51.170
for this person, we're gonna work retraining.

646
00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:52.720
So somebody did this,

647
00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:55.490
let's say their max is on this, 225 kilos,

648
00:22:55.490 --> 00:22:58.745
which is about 496 in pounds, if you work in pounds.

649
00:22:58.745 --> 00:23:03.230
140 kilos on bench, that's about 308 pounds, I believe.

650
00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:06.381
And then deadlift 230 which I believe is 507 pounds.

651
00:23:06.381 --> 00:23:10.540
So this person's pretty strong, pretty good base,

652
00:23:10.540 --> 00:23:12.410
they've definitely been training for a while.

653
00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:13.920
And so if they have these numbers,

654
00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:15.300
and it's important to remember those numbers

655
00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:18.540
because we're gonna use some lower percentages of those

656
00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:19.470
as we're working back.

657
00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:21.610
So, this is what this person was doing,

658
00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:23.140
pretty high-volume program,

659
00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:24.545
training the lifts frequently,

660
00:23:24.545 --> 00:23:27.940
training a lot, putting a lot of assistance work in there,

661
00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:30.210
and so let's see how we would retraining.

662
00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:32.780
So they did this and then they had three months off,

663
00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:34.360
and now we're gonna write a three-month program

664
00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:35.680
to get 'em back.

665
00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:37.500
So the first two weeks:

666
00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:39.330
just do something.

667
00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:41.100
Nothing strenuous at all.

668
00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:43.160
These are your very first two weeks back

669
00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:44.680
from taking three months off.

670
00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:45.830
And it's one thing to say

671
00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:47.140
that we take three months off,

672
00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:49.005
but actually think about it for a moment.

673
00:23:49.005 --> 00:23:53.230
You did not touch a weight for three months.

674
00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:55.080
That's a long time.

675
00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:57.090
Even if you didn't lose as much size and strength

676
00:23:57.090 --> 00:24:00.100
as you thought, you're really gonna be susceptible

677
00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:01.580
to fatigue when you start back.

678
00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:04.760
So, we're only gonna train three total days

679
00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:06.063
in the first two weeks.

680
00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:08.530
Each week is gonna be three days,

681
00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:10.600
so six total days for two weeks.

682
00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:12.120
But three training days per week.

683
00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:13.930
You could definitely get away with less than that

684
00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:15.070
I should say as well.

685
00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:16.656
Even if you only train once or twice.

686
00:24:16.656 --> 00:24:19.310
But a frequency of once on the main lifts

687
00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:20.400
in the first week,

688
00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:22.000
and then getting to a frequency of twice

689
00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:23.133
on the main lifts in the second week

690
00:24:23.133 --> 00:24:25.900
is what we're gonna do in this first couple of weeks.

691
00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:27.690
And then, as we said earlier,

692
00:24:27.690 --> 00:24:29.240
we're not gonna use percentages,

693
00:24:29.240 --> 00:24:31.590
we're gonna program absolute loads in week one,

694
00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:33.120
and absolute loads in week two

695
00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:34.540
and be really conservative.

696
00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:35.373
But you're gonna see,

697
00:24:35.373 --> 00:24:37.391
even when we say twice a week in week two,

698
00:24:37.391 --> 00:24:39.010
it's really easy.

699
00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:41.103
So remember, previously this individual

700
00:24:41.103 --> 00:24:46.103
could max squat 225 kilos, bench 140, and deadlift 230.

701
00:24:46.893 --> 00:24:48.710
Now, our first week back,

702
00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:51.730
so week one is up here, I'll circle it here with the cursor.

703
00:24:51.730 --> 00:24:53.230
Training three times per week,

704
00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:54.980
you can see squatting just once,

705
00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:55.813
benching just once,

706
00:24:55.813 --> 00:24:57.560
deadlifting just once.

707
00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:59.949
They could squat 225 kilos the first week back,

708
00:24:59.949 --> 00:25:02.890
three sets of five, 65 kilograms.

709
00:25:02.890 --> 00:25:04.290
I know it's absurdly light,

710
00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:05.773
I know they could do a lot more,

711
00:25:05.773 --> 00:25:10.320
even if they could only max 170 kilos

712
00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:12.318
and they used to be able to do 225,

713
00:25:12.318 --> 00:25:15.327
65 kilos is exactly 65 more kilos

714
00:25:15.327 --> 00:25:18.530
than they had squatted any day after the first three months.

715
00:25:18.530 --> 00:25:20.605
So it's a really really light load,

716
00:25:20.605 --> 00:25:23.570
this is off whatever their projected new max would be

717
00:25:23.570 --> 00:25:24.890
which we'll look at in a moment.

718
00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:27.240
This is 30%-ish, something like that,

719
00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:29.355
maybe a little bit less, absurdly light.

720
00:25:29.355 --> 00:25:32.720
The assistance work is staying at a five RPE or less

721
00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:34.800
just a couple sets of rows here,

722
00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:36.910
again even though this is really light,

723
00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:38.860
it might cause some fatigue and soreness

724
00:25:38.860 --> 00:25:40.220
for the next couple days

725
00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:41.911
'cause they have literally done nothing.

726
00:25:41.911 --> 00:25:44.400
Bench press again, working at 50 kilos,

727
00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:46.060
they could have done 140 before,

728
00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:48.440
really light, just putting a couple assistance movements

729
00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:50.666
in there, nothing strenuous at all.

730
00:25:50.666 --> 00:25:53.167
Deadlift just singles at 70 kilos,

731
00:25:53.167 --> 00:25:55.350
and they could deadlift 230, right?

732
00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:59.050
They're just doing one rep and no collar on the bar.

733
00:25:59.050 --> 00:26:00.860
Some leg extensions and they're just one set

734
00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:02.030
of lateral raises.

735
00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:05.684
Really really easy, it looks like you're not doing anything,

736
00:26:05.684 --> 00:26:08.060
but the goal of the first two weeks

737
00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:09.910
was just do something.

738
00:26:09.910 --> 00:26:12.410
I'm gonna hit on this next refrain

739
00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:13.360
a few more times,

740
00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:15.930
but when you're doing something like this,

741
00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:18.210
you're going to want to do more, right?

742
00:26:18.210 --> 00:26:19.180
If you've taken time off,

743
00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:20.750
and usually it only takes one or two days,

744
00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:23.010
and then you're motivated and you wanna do more,

745
00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:24.730
don't, right?

746
00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:26.900
Only bad things can come from that.

747
00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:29.380
If you follow something gradual after taking time off,

748
00:26:29.380 --> 00:26:30.550
it's going to work.

749
00:26:30.550 --> 00:26:31.690
You gotta trust the process

750
00:26:31.690 --> 00:26:32.540
and you gotta stick with it,

751
00:26:32.540 --> 00:26:33.603
and it's gonna work.

752
00:26:33.603 --> 00:26:36.090
If you wanna jump up and do more,

753
00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:37.600
just because you can do it,

754
00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:39.180
doesn't mean you should do it.

755
00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:40.740
So from there, we go into week two,

756
00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:42.670
and in week two, you can see on squat,

757
00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:45.251
it went from 65 kilos to 80 kilos here.

758
00:26:45.251 --> 00:26:47.720
Now you can see at the end of the week,

759
00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:49.590
we've added a second day of squatting.

760
00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:53.320
These six reps at 80 kilos probably aren't gonna cause

761
00:26:53.320 --> 00:26:54.290
much soreness at all,

762
00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:56.144
'cause we've elicited the repeated bout effect now,

763
00:26:56.144 --> 00:26:58.230
and also what we've done is we've tried

764
00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:00.452
to quote "protect against muscle damage."

765
00:27:00.452 --> 00:27:02.680
So when you look at the repeated bout effect,

766
00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:04.230
by performing such a light load,

767
00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:07.508
what that does is it causes a minimal amount of damage

768
00:27:07.508 --> 00:27:10.070
but just enough so that way

769
00:27:10.070 --> 00:27:12.380
then ultimately there's recovery of skeletal muscle

770
00:27:12.380 --> 00:27:14.080
and there's repair of skeletal muscle,

771
00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:16.280
and it's more resistant to fatigue here.

772
00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:18.670
So we'd also call that protecting against damage.

773
00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:20.770
So now we've added a second day of squatting in here,

774
00:27:20.770 --> 00:27:22.880
but it's still absurdly light.

775
00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:26.177
It's just doubles here, working at a four to five RPE,

776
00:27:26.177 --> 00:27:29.490
and then is just a guide that's working up to 100 kilos.

777
00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:31.610
The reason I put this in here is,

778
00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:34.350
I would ask this person to cap it at 100 kilos,

779
00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:36.850
so even if they're double at a four to five RPE

780
00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:38.780
was above that, it gives them a cap,

781
00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:40.327
say "Hey, don't go above that,

782
00:27:40.327 --> 00:27:42.220
"that's as much as I want you to do."

783
00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:44.160
so in week two, we're still working

784
00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:46.350
at absurdly light loads here,

785
00:27:46.350 --> 00:27:48.750
we've added a second day of bench pressing here as well,

786
00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:51.711
you can see bumping up to 65 kilos on the sixes day.

787
00:27:51.711 --> 00:27:53.100
And then some doubles here,

788
00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:55.630
capping them at 85 kilos on the bench,

789
00:27:55.630 --> 00:27:58.030
remember again they could bench 140 before.

790
00:27:58.030 --> 00:27:59.340
So this is still really really light,

791
00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:01.247
and even though we've gone to a frequency of twice a week,

792
00:28:01.247 --> 00:28:04.703
it's not really a full frequency of twice a week,

793
00:28:04.703 --> 00:28:07.450
it's still absurdly light, here on this day.

794
00:28:07.450 --> 00:28:09.860
And this other day is just doubles at really light loads

795
00:28:09.860 --> 00:28:12.740
as well, and then we have a little more assistance work,

796
00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:14.130
we've added a set of curls here,

797
00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:15.950
