and I think there are some really cool strategies

1332
00:46:24.710 --> 00:46:25.660
coming back from injury

1333
00:46:25.660 --> 00:46:28.970
whether it's around manipulating the movement

1334
00:46:28.970 --> 00:46:31.470
and doing, if you can't squat,

1335
00:46:31.470 --> 00:46:32.620
doing a box squat,

1336
00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:35.040
or doing some portion of the movement

1337
00:46:35.040 --> 00:46:37.120
that you can, and then when you can do the movement,

1338
00:46:37.120 --> 00:46:38.433
using a lighter load.

1339
00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:40.561
Working on slow eccentrics

1340
00:46:40.561 --> 00:46:42.760
to make sure you're not overloading too much

1341
00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:44.650
in the early stages of coming back.

1342
00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:46.730
You know, the biopsychosocial model

1343
00:46:46.730 --> 00:46:48.620
of pain is very interesting,

1344
00:46:48.620 --> 00:46:50.910
however it's not something I consider myself

1345
00:46:50.910 --> 00:46:52.780
to be an expert on, I'm not a physical therapist.

1346
00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:54.646
So I just wanna say that

1347
00:46:54.646 --> 00:46:56.320
'cause I don't want anybody watching this to think

1348
00:46:56.320 --> 00:46:58.060
that this applies to everything

1349
00:46:58.060 --> 00:46:59.960
or it applies to someone coming back from an injury.

1350
00:46:59.960 --> 00:47:01.260
If you're coming back from an injury,

1351
00:47:01.260 --> 00:47:03.640
you're certainly gonna probably have to modify exercises,

1352
00:47:03.640 --> 00:47:05.260
